  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing variances, which involves assessing the spread of outcomes around the expected value. While both lotteries have the same expected value (2700), the individual does not refer to any concept related to dispersion or variance. Instead, the individual focuses on the salience of the 90% chance in A and the unattractiveness of probabilities below 50% in B. This reflects a heuristic based on probability weighting or threshold-based reasoning (e.g., favoring 'high probability wins'), not a calculation or consideration of variance. Although the individual ends up choosing the lottery with lower variance (A), the reasoning process does not align with the variance-based rule. The information considered (probability levels, psychological appeal of >90%) differs from the variance-relevant information (outcome deviations weighted by probabilities). The justification is based on probability magnitude, not outcome variability. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used variance as a decision criterion.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
